        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.942,
            0.867,
            0.969
          ],
          "content": "— Textbook of Science for Grade 8 mwww"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.093,
            0.65,
            0.117
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.6: Let us construct"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.139,
            0.397,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.15,
            0.383,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "Can we also make our own Voltaic cell using easily a<u>vailable materials?</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.136,
            0.883,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "z Take five or six juicy lemons, copper wires/ strips (1–2 mm thick) and iron nails. Also take one LED and some connecting wires."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.417,
            0.198,
            0.883,
            0.254
          ],
          "content": "z Insert the copper wire and the iron nail in one of the lemons keeping them apart by a small distance as shown in Fig. 4.8a."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.248,
            0.342,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.201,
                0.4,
                0.227,
                0.415
              ],
              "content": "(a)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.258,
            0.882,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "z Repeat the above step for all the remaining lemons."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.282,
            0.883,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "z Join the copper wires and nails as shown in Fig. 4.8b."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.325,
            0.883,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "z Connect the LED between the copper wire of the first lemon and the iron nail of the last lemon, using connecting wires. What do you observe? Does the LED glow?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.047,
            0.403,
            0.576,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.145,
                0.577,
                0.493,
                0.609
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.8: (a) Electric cell made using lemons; (b) Connections in lemon cell"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.586,
            0.404,
            0.885,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "z If the LED does not glow, reverse its connections. Does the LED glow now? [Remember that we have learnt earlier that current can pass through the LED only when the positive terminal (longer wire) of the LED is connected to the positive terminal of the battery, and negative terminal (shorter wire) of"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.625,
            0.882,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "the LED is connected to the negative terminal of the battery]."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.649,
            0.883,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "A glowing LED indicates that your cell is working. In this cell, the metal electrodes are the copper wires and the iron nails. The electrolyte is the lemon juice, which helps conduct electricity. You may also use salt solutions instead of lemon juice."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
            0.757,
            0.25,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.737,
            0.426,
            0.758
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.775,
            0.857,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "Some common metal pairs for Voltaic cells are zinc/copper, zinc/ silver, aluminium/copper, iron/copper, magnesium/copper, and lead/copper. Some metals—like copper—act as positive electrodes, yet some other metals—like zinc—act as negative electrodes. This is due to their chemical properties. We will learn more about this in the higher grades."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
            0.924,
            0.094,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": "56"
        },
